From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Mon, 13 Jul 2026 08:34:15 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 06945/11362] [outer-loop] task 11 (partial): goal-hypothesis
 consistency check against real transitions

Task 11 of ops/known-issues.md's 2026-07-11 SOTA-ingestion queue
(hallucination-consistency checks). Two independent top-3 ARC-AGI-3 teams
each carry an unexploited self-report-vs-ground-truth gap: Reki's
board_change_assessment (what it thinks changed) is never cross-checked
against changed_pixels (the real pixel diff); Duck's free-text Goal/Action
hypothesis is regenerated each turn but never checked against observed
level-up/no-change transitions -- both a literal, unexploited instance of
this project's founding thesis sitting inside two winning pipelines.

Investigating our own architecture found no direct analog to Reki's exact
natural-language self-report (not built this session), but found the
DYNAMICS half of the same gap-class was already closed
(WorldModelVerifier.score(engine) checks the induced engine()'s predicted
next-grid against the real observed next-grid) while the GOAL half was
genuinely open: nothing validated is_level_complete (the induced code's
formalized goal hypothesis, installed by execute_bounded_llm_reinduction as
a search termination condition) against real observed level-progress
ground truth.

Added score_goal_predicate_consistency/GoalPredicateConsistency to
arc_executable_world_model.py -- the goal-hypothesis sibling of
WorldModelVerifier/VerifyResult. For each transition, real ground truth is
level_after > level_before; the claim under test is
is_level_complete(next_grid). A cheap, deterministic sign check, no second
LLM call, matching forge's own competitive-pressure finding that an
expensive LLM judge was not worth the cost. Purely additive: no change to
WorldModelVerifier, VerifyResult, or any existing induction/planning
behavior; not yet wired into any live decision (e.g. vetoing a goal
predicate before planning) -- a distinct, separately-scoped design +
validation step, same discipline as the color-blob salience topology
extension.

Validated by 5 direct unit tests on realistic (non-toy) synthetic data: a
perfect predictor scores 1.0; a predictor that never claims a win is CAUGHT
missing a real level-up; a predictor that claims every state is a win is
CAUGHT false-positiving a no-op (both miscalibration directions detected);
a raising predicate is handled gracefully, never propagates; an empty
transition list returns a well-formed zero result, no ZeroDivisionError.

The offline-sim prototype (exp5593) attempted a REAL end-to-end test
against lp85 (the only game with any measured headroom across this
session's A/Bs) using the real default Qwen3.5-9B-MTP proposer, and found a
genuine, precisely-diagnosed pre-existing limitation of the induce_prompt/
LocalGGUFProposer machinery: lp85's 64x64 logical grid makes the fixed
"render the full initial grid" prompt overhead alone consume most of the
induction pipeline's 13,824-token available budget. Confirmed by direct
debugging (bypassing LocalGGUFProposer.induce()'s own truncated
error-repr handling to read the llama-server's actual JSON error body): an
8-transition window measured 18,355 prompt tokens (exceed_context_size_
error, n_ctx=16384); even a minimal single-transition window (the level-up
transition alone) measured ~13,400+ tokens -- already at the edge
regardless of transition count. Induction never produced a real predicate
to score; this is a finding about the shared pipeline's large-grid
scalability, not a flaw in the new consistency-check function itself.
Fixing induce_prompt's large-grid scalability is out of scope for this
task. adversarial_verify.py's DURATION_TOO_SHORT flag disclosed honestly
(flagged_adversarial=true + methodology_note), same pattern as exp5588/
5589/5590.

Regression-checked via a smaller, targeted subset after the original
59-file sweep deadlocked in xdist (a pre-existing, unrelated parallel-
execution issue -- confirmed by successfully running a smaller subset
clean, and separately confirming the one flagged memory-leak teardown
error is on an unrelated pre-existing test, id 4494, that leaks the exact
same ~520-540MB even run in complete isolation with zero exposure to any
new code added here).

7 new tests total (5 direct unit tests for score_goal_predicate_
consistency; 6 for the exp5593 prototype script covering blocked-
precondition, both SCENARIO outcome classes, the no-real-levelup
inconclusive case, context-overflow classification, and the checked-in
real-run artifact).

